WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.147
Now let's talk about exceptions.

00:00:02.147 --> 00:00:08.877
An exception is Python's way of saying that something went wrong while the program was running.

00:00:08.877 --> 00:00:14.092
For example, Python may raise an error if we try to convert the text N A into a

00:00:14.092 --> 00:00:19.807
number, or if we try to divide by zero, or if a dictionary does not contain the key

00:00:19.807 --> 00:00:21.492
we expected.

00:00:21.492 --> 00:00:27.722
In professional code, we do not want the entire workflow to fail just because one expected messy value

00:00:27.722 --> 00:00:30.662
appears in the data. But we also do not want to hide every error.

00:00:30.662 --> 00:00:36.077
This distinction is important. Some problems are expected.

00:00:36.077 --> 00:00:40.272
For example, a dataset may contain missing values or invalid numeric entries.

00:00:40.272 --> 00:00:42.287
We can handle those safely.

00:00:42.287 --> 00:00:45.007
Other problems are unexpected.

00:00:45.007 --> 00:00:50.867
For example, if our function has the wrong logic, we should fix it instead of hiding the error.

00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:56.122
Good exception handling catches a specific error and responds in a clear way.

00:00:56.122 --> 00:01:02.960
The pattern is: try to do something, catch the known problem, and return a safe result or message.
